<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<odoo>
    <!--
        =====================================================================
        WORKAROUND: OCA `dms` 18.0 refuses EVERYBODY the right to create a
        directory or a file, including administrators.
        =====================================================================

        THE BUG
        =======
        `dms/security/security.xml` declares four GLOBAL record rules per
        model that compare a BOOLEAN field with a USER ID:

            <field name="domain_force">
                [('permission_create', '=', user.id)]
            </field>

        `permission_create` is a `fields.Boolean` (computed from the
        directory's access groups). `user.id` is an integer. `True == 2` is
        false, so the rule denies every record to every user, always.

        OCA knows about this and works around it in
        `dms/models/dms_security_mixin.py`:

            def _filtered_access(self, operation):
                result = super()._filtered_access(operation)
                # HACK Always fall back to applying rules by SQL.
                # Upstream `_filtered_access()` doesn't use computed fields
                # search methods. Thus, it will take the
                # `[('permission_{operation}', '=', user.id)]` rule literally.
                # Obviously that will always fail because
                # `self[f"permission_{operation}"]` will always be a `bool`,
                # while `user.id` will always be an `int`.
                result |= self._filtered_access_no_recursion(operation)
                return result

        But `create()` does not go through `_filtered_access()`. It goes
        through `create()` -> `_check_access_dms_record("create")` ->
        `ir.rule._compute_domain()`, which evaluates the rule literally. The
        HACK never runs on that path.

        Verified 2026-11-05 in a fresh database with `dms` + `dms_webdav`:

            sub.permission_create(admin)            -> True
            Dms.search(create-domain) finds sub     -> False
            Dms.create(...) as admin                -> AccessError

        The field and the rule disagree, and the rule wins.

        WHY THIS IS IN OUR MODULE AND NOT IN `dms`
        ==========================================
        Patching an OCA module in place would be overwritten on the next
        `repo_apply`. A small override here is visible, reversible, and
        disappears the day upstream fixes it.

        WHAT THIS DOES
        ==============
        Rewrites the eight rules to compare the boolean with `True`. The rules
        keep their purpose — "you may only touch a record whose computed
        permission field says so" — they just stop comparing a boolean to an
        integer.

        ⚠️ THIS IS A WORKAROUND, NOT A FIX. If upstream changes the rule name
        or the field, this fails loudly at upgrade (the `ref` will not
        resolve), which is what we want: a silent no-op would leave the bug
        in place.
    -->

    <!-- Directories -->
    <record id="dms.rule_directory_computed_create" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_create', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_directory_computed_read" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_read', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_directory_computed_write" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_write', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_directory_computed_unlink" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_unlink', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>

    <!-- Files -->
    <record id="dms.rule_file_computed_create" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_create', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_file_computed_read" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_read', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_file_computed_write" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_write', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
    <record id="dms.rule_file_computed_unlink" model="ir.rule">
        <field name="domain_force">[('permission_unlink', '=', True)]</field>
    </record>
</odoo>
